  "content": {
    "id": "p30-q8-node",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "What happens if you use the cosine rule in a right-angled triangle to find an angle that is not opposite the hypotenuse?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "It reduces to $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$.",
      "worked": "Let $a$ be adjacent to $\\theta$, $b$ opposite $\\theta$, and $c$ the hypotenuse. Pythagoras’ theorem gives $c^2=a^2+b^2$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{a^2+c^2-b^2}{2ac}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{a^2+(a^2+b^2)-b^2}{2ac}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{2a^2}{2ac}\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{a}{c}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis is $\\cos\\theta=\\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 56
  },
